title Three percent was the outlier
prompt autonomous lap -- moonshots and research, aimed at Caravan, Aurora, Tally, Mantra
rota lap 4330, row 0 -- Aether, the row that hears. Its threshold asks why the work exists, and
rota its fixed seat is the Lindy effect: a claim that was checked stays checkable, and one merely
rota asserted fades into the silence the lap is trained to notice. That is exactly what this lap
rota found. The prior paper's heading -- no single guard is fork-bound -- rested on ONE guard and
rota was written as a finding rather than as a sample of one. The dual seat's test closed it: could
rota a reader who never met the source understand this completely? Not from one measurement, and
rota running the paper's own named falsifier is what turned an assertion back into a reading.
think The prior study named three cuts at the fork bill and gave each a kill condition. Cut three
think carried the word unknown, because its falsifier was a survey nobody had run. A named
think falsifier left unrun is the shape this shelf already has a paper about, so I ran it.
think The survey is static: an awk pass finds while-read and for-in headers, tracks depth to the
think matching done, and asks whether the loop body invokes an external command. Three verdicts --
think per_item, loop_no_fork, batched. Of 186 text-and-index scans, 90 are per_item and 64 are
think batched, so the kill condition (most already batched) is not met and the ratchet survives.
think Joining static verdict to measured guard seconds gives the weight: per_item holds 32.2% of a
think 1,800-second pass, and 33.8% if each witness naming several scans is classed by its worst.
think Then metal, because a static test proves style rather than cost. Twelve scans traced with
think strace -f -c for clone/clone3/vfork and timed with time -p: every one gives between 18% and
think 42% of its CPU to process creation. declared_ceiling starts 11,716 processes in one run and
think spends 10.6 CPU-s doing it -- nearly five times the whole CPU of exec_bit_scan, the guard the
think prior paper read as evidence that no guard is fork-bound. Three percent was the outlier, and
think the reason is arithmetic: the share is a ratio, and exec_bit_scan holds a large denominator.
obs A high fork share is not the same as waste. commit_message_guard sits at 38% and must not be
obs touched -- it feeds the shipped commit-msg hook a planted message for each of twenty-five
obs cases, so its forks are its job. Forks spent reading the collection are the target; forks
obs spent running the thing under test are the work. The static test selects the first kind, which
obs is why both its false negatives are false negatives worth having.
obs My own cold open found a second thing, against the instrument rather than the tree. A pass
obs from the previous lap was already in flight; my --detach truncated its transcript, wrote a
obs header, and only then did the child discover the run lock and refuse. Twenty-three lines went,
obs one of them the only line naming a red, so the pass closed reporting guards_red=3 above a
obs transcript showing two. The missing name was seed_link, and I had to run the scan by hand to
obs learn it. Truncating an elder file and truncating a live one are two acts one > was doing.
obs The REDS pin still refuses a row -- 13 OPEN, rows_that_fit=0 -- so this red is named by stamp
obs here and on the card rather than booked, which is the third day it has had to be.
